Ron stepped into the office and closed the fire door behind him.

“You assembled that conversation from separate moments,” he said.

“The components happened,” Evan answered.

“On different floors.”

“Within the same investigation.”

“Before and after you knew the phrase.”

“That matters to us, not to the source.”

Diane watched the timeline. Each cut imposed rhythm on uncertain material. Evan had shortened gaps, lowered noise, and placed the piano note between phrases like punctuation. By putting Wayne’s incomplete file after a spoken name, he made it function as a response.

“You made it answer you,” she said.

“I revealed the answer.”

“You selected a question from one hour and placed it beside a sound from another.”

“Documentaries compress time.”

“Compression changes duration. This changes causality.”

Evan folded his arms. “Then explain the new raw phrase.”

“I already explained the echo.”

“You explained the first repetition. There is another sound after it.”

“An ambiguous sound.”

“That matches the route.”

“After you named the route.”

Abby removed the copied memory card from the office computer and placed it inside a clear evidence sleeve. “I could not hear ‘up here’ before the label.”

“You hear it now,” Evan said.

“I hear what you trained me to hear.”

Diane reacted more strongly to the word trained than she expected.

Evan looked toward the dark auditorium window. “So we need a cleaner source.”

Ron shook his head. “We need to leave.”

“A locked room, one camera, no people, no questions, no live monitoring speaker. Ten minutes.”

“No,” Diane said.

“The booth is already the final point in Wayne’s route.”

“The booth is a workspace full of wiring, loose fixtures, old control boards, radio interference, temperature changes, and access routes you have not mapped.”

“Then map them.”

“That requires daylight, records, and staff.”

“We can establish the room empty, lock the door, keep the key on camera, and monitor from the stage.”

“You already turned incomplete controls into certainty.”

“Then you control this one.”

Diane looked at him.

An unsupervised test was the only argument likely to move her. It would become another contaminated source. By supervising the test, she would authorize the very decision she wanted to forbid.

Ron spoke before she answered. “Diane.”

“No,” she said, though the word addressed herself as much as Evan.

Evan rotated the laptop toward her. “The question is simple. If the pattern is editing, the empty booth gives us room tone. If the pattern follows the route, the booth gives us the next event. Either result helps.”

“Those are not the only results.”

“Name another.”

“Someone enters through a maintenance access. The camera drops frames. The wireless feed delays. A cable moves. A control relay clicks. Sound from the house reaches the microphone. Your software fills a corrupted frame. Battery heat changes focus. Dust crosses the infrared light. We spend the night arguing about a blur.”

“Then we document each condition.”

Ron took the booth key from the wall board. “You have no authorization for this.”

Evan pointed toward the permission sheet in his bag. “Selected auditorium spaces under supervision.”

“The lighting booth contains controlled equipment,” Diane said.

“So you supervise.”

Abby stepped away from the table. “This stopped being a school project in the basement.”

Evan’s expression tightened. “You can leave the camera with us.”

“No.”

“You want to stop now because the evidence became personal.”

“I want to stop because Ron withdrew, Diane set an end point, and you keep treating every boundary as another dramatic obstacle.”

The room fell quiet.

Beyond the fire door, the auditorium work lights shut off on their timer.

The window went dark. The office lights reflected four figures in the glass, layered over the faint red exit signs inside the house.

A relay clicked somewhere above the stage.

Evan looked toward it.

Diane remained facing the reflection. “Lighting timer. The booth carries the control circuit.”

“Which makes the location active,” Evan said.

“Which makes it contaminated.”

Another click followed. A row of aisle lights dimmed, then returned.

Ron placed the booth key on the table. “I am finished. I will stay long enough to walk the students to the lobby. After that, I am logging out.”

Evan lowered his voice. “You heard breathing again.”

Ron’s face closed.

“That gives you no claim on him,” Diane said.

“It gives the pattern another repetition.”

“It gives a tired man an experience in a mechanical building after you spent an hour telling him what the building was doing.”

Ron opened the office door. “Five minutes.”

He stepped into the backstage corridor but remained visible through the doorway.

Evan returned to the raw camera file. “One more thing.”

Diane’s patience thinned. “No more edited sequences.”

“This is untouched.”

He loaded the camera track from the moment after the three piano notes. On the recording, Diane closed the fallboard, Evan asked why those keys mattered, and Ron told him to leave the question alone.

Evan placed the playhead in the silence after Diane named Wayne’s route.

The office speakers released the corridor’s air noise.

A small sound crossed the right channel.

Two syllables, or one scrape divided by automatic gain.

Evan played it again and supplied the phrase before anyone else spoke.

“Lock it.”

Diane listened without moving.

Abby said, “I hear ‘look in.’”

Ron, from the corridor, said, “I hear a zipper.”

Evan increased the volume.

The sound widened. A clothing rasp preceded it. The camera motor shifted. Somewhere nearby, a key or buckle touched metal.

“Lock it,” Evan repeated.

Diane heard the phrase on the fourth playback.

She also heard Abby’s camera-bag zipper from moments earlier in the scene, Ron’s key ring, and a brief fragment of Evan saying lighting booth before the group left the stairs. The file contained enough related sounds to support the phrase and enough contamination to make that reading unreliable.

“When was this label created?” Diane asked.

“Just now.”

“When was the clip isolated?”

“After we entered the office.”

“Who heard it before you named it?”

Evan gave no answer.

“Then it proves nothing,” she said.

“It suggests the same test.”

“It suggests your attention has narrowed around one destination.”

“The destination was on Wayne’s route.”

“And on your note before you found this sound.”

Evan looked at the timeline marker: NEXT TEST: LOCKED BOOTH.

He had planned the booth test before finding the sound he now used to justify it.

For a moment, he looked uncertain.

Then a faint metallic knock sounded from the auditorium.

One strike.

A pause.

Another.

The booth door stood above the rear seats, beyond the office wall and dark house. The sound could have come from its closer, a lighting pipe, a cooling relay, the stage door, or a person crossing the upper corridor.

Evan closed the laptop.

“Let us rule out the booth,” he said.

Diane understood the trap in the wording. Refusal would harden his certainty. Agreement would legitimize the test. Confiscation would send him back with a phone, a borrowed camera, or students free from her controls.

She looked at Abby. “Will you follow my procedure?”

Abby nodded. “One continuous setup. No edited prompts. No one alone.”

Diane looked at Ron.

He shook his head once. “I want them out.”

“So do I.”

“Then take them out.”

“If I end it here, he returns without us.”

Evan offered no denial.

Ron’s expression hardened toward him, then softened toward Diane. “Ten minutes becomes twenty. One room becomes the crawlspace.”

“Ten minutes,” Diane said. “The booth is inspected in full light. One camera. Internal recording and a delayed monitor only. No recorder, no phone audio, no questions, no names, no playback into the room. The camera faces the door and the glassless opening. The booth key remains with me. Everyone stays together on the stage. At the first broken condition, the test ends.”

Evan opened the laptop again to write.

Diane closed it with one hand.

“You record the conditions on paper.”

Abby tore a clean page from the auditorium log pad.

Ron stared at the key on the table. “And after ten minutes?”

“We remove the camera, lock the booth, and escort them out.”

“You promise?”

Diane looked through the office window at the dark house.

The booth opening appeared above the seats like an empty frame.

“I take responsibility,” she said.

Diane’s answer was not a promise.

Ron heard the difference.

He picked up the key and placed it in Diane’s palm.

Its long steel shaft felt colder than the other keys on her ring.

Evan gathered the camera bag.

Abby kept the write-protected source card sealed in her pocket and loaded a fresh card into the camera. She stated the card number, battery level, internal clock, and lens settings while Diane wrote them down.

They left the office together.

At the booth stairs, Diane unlocked the door and entered first.

The room smelled of dust, warm plastic, and old fabric. Two control boards occupied the front console. Bundled cables ran along the walls. Two gray chairs stood behind the operator position, ordinary and dry, the pair assigned to the booth since Diane’s inventory.

Diane counted both, then carried them onto the landing while Abby filmed each move. An empty floor would remove one source of scraping and leave the booth camera a clear view of the door.

She checked the maintenance hatch, the ceiling access panel, the door closer, the glassless opening, the outlets, and the dead space behind the dimmer rack. Abby filmed each location without cutting.

Evan remained on the landing where Diane had placed him.

When the inspection ended, Abby set the camera on a tripod facing the door and booth opening. A cable carried a delayed image to Evan’s laptop on the stage below. Internal audio remained active. Every other device stayed outside.

Diane backed through the door.

Abby filmed the empty booth from the landing, then handed the camera to Diane long enough to show Abby, Evan, and Ron together.

Ron raised one hand toward the lens. “I asked for the session to end. I am leaving before this test begins.”

Diane held his gaze. Earlier, she had treated his refusal as another condition to document. This time she stepped aside.

Ron left through the stage door. Its closer pulled it shut with a slow hydraulic sigh. The empty space beside Abby remained in frame.

Diane returned the camera, closed the booth door, and turned the key.

The lock engaged with one firm movement.

She removed the key and held it before Abby’s lens.

“Five seventeen,” Abby said. “Locked-booth test begins.”

From inside the booth came the faint sound of a chair leg settling against the floor.

No one spoke.

Diane closed her hand around the key.

“Stage,” she said. “All together.”

# **Scene 8 — The Glassless Window**

Diane placed the monitor on a road case at center stage and turned its screen toward the house.

The booth feed showed a green-gray rectangle above the empty seats. Abby’s camera stood inside the locked room on a tripod facing the glassless opening. Its wide lens held the control console, the door, and a section of bare floor in one frame. A timecode ran along the lower edge. At setup, the digits trailed the main camera by roughly seven-tenths of a second, close enough for ordinary monitoring and wide enough to confuse any fast movement.

Diane wrote the delay on a strip of gaffer tape and fixed it to the road case.

“Main camera stays continuous,” she said. “Monitor, stage, and all three of us remain in frame. Phones stay on the table. No questions, names, playback, or sound cues.”

Evan set his phone beside the laptop. “The booth camera has its own microphone.”

“And its own memory card.”

“Freshly formatted.”

“Abby recorded the format screen.”

Abby stood ten feet away with the main camera on her shoulder. Its frame included Diane beside the road case, Evan near the laptop, the booth opening above the rear seats, and both phones on the table. She read the time aloud.

